#!/bin/sh
# open-anesthesia-sim git hook: installed from .claude/hooks/git/ by stop_hook_patch.py.
#
# Refuse any deletion of a remote-tracking ref in a session's clone (`PL-82J5`,
# `PL-1ZZ0`). Git runs this hook with each ref a command is about to change, one
# line each: the old value, the new value and the ref's name. A deletion's new
# value is all zeros. In the `prepared` state a non-zero exit aborts the whole
# change, so git fails having changed no ref.
#
# **Why here and not on the command's words.** A stale `origin/<branch>` ref can
# be the only surviving copy of an item captured on a branch nobody merged, and
# `bin/docket stranded` is what recovers it. The Bash guard this replaces read
# each command's words to find a prune, and every spelling its model of bash and
# git lacked deleted refs unread: its own table listed ten (`PL-61FT`, `PL-82J5`).
# This hook sees what git is about to do, so the spelling does not matter.
# Measured with git 2.43.0 on 2026-11-03: `fetch --prune`, `-c fetch.prune=true
# fetch`, `pull --prune`, `remote update --prune`, `remote prune`, `branch -dr`,
# `update-ref -d`, `update-ref --stdin`, `remote remove` and `remote rename` were
# all refused with every ref intact, while a commit, a local branch's deletion,
# `pack-refs --all` and `gc` ran as before.
#
# **Only an agent's git is refused.** Claude Code sets `CLAUDECODE` in the
# environment of every command it runs, and git hands that environment to its
# hooks. The project owner's own git in the same clone is left alone, which is
# the scope the Bash guard had.
#
# **What goes unread.** A command that switches hooks off, such as `-c
# core.hooksPath=/dev/null`, and a file removed by hand under `.git/refs` or
# rewritten in `.git/packed-refs`. `git remote rename` renames the remote's
# settings before it moves any ref, so a refused rename leaves the settings
# renamed and every ref where it was.
#
# **Nothing else refuses these deletions** (`PL-39Y1`). The refs are in the
# session's own clone, which GitHub never sees, so no rule on GitHub's side can
# stand behind this hook. `tools/github_refusals_check.py` lists a prune that
# skips the hook among the actions nothing refuses.
#
# Edit the copy in `.claude/hooks/git/`. The installed copy is overwritten at
# the next session start.

[ "$1" = prepared ] || exit 0
[ -n "${CLAUDECODE:-}" ] || exit 0

refs=
while read -r old new ref; do
    case $new in
    *[!0]*) ;;
    *)
        case $ref in
        refs/remotes/*) refs="$refs
    $ref" ;;
        esac
        ;;
    esac
done
[ -n "$refs" ] || exit 0

python3 .claude/hooks/hook_input.py --log git-ref-guard delete-tracking-ref >/dev/null 2>&1

cat >&2 <<EOF
Refused: this command would delete these remote-tracking refs, and git has changed no ref:
$refs

A remote-tracking ref can be the only surviving copy of an item captured on a
branch nobody merged, so no command in a session may delete one (PL-82J5).
Run \`bin/docket stranded\` to see what such refs hold.

- To fetch, leave out the prune: \`git fetch origin\`. If you did not ask for a
  prune, a setting did. \`git config --show-origin --get-regexp prune\` names
  it, and \`git config --unset <name>\` clears it.
- To restart a branch whose pull request has merged, move the branch, carry
  over any commit the merge did not take, and push. The push moves the stale
  ref, so it needs no deleting:

      git fetch origin main
      git checkout -B <branch> origin/main
      git cherry-pick <commit>
      git push -u origin <branch>

  Where the remote still holds the old branch, git rejects that push as
  behind it. Never force that push: GitHub refuses a forced push on every
  branch (PL-39Y1). Read what the old branch holds, carry across any commit
  that is not here, then ask the project owner to archive and delete the old
  branch, and push again once it is gone. \`bin/docket branch\` prints the
  steps.

- To point a remote somewhere else, keep it and its refs:
  \`git remote set-url <name> <url>\`.
EOF
exit 1
